14:14:37 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Fair point about how real conversations work — most people say "Bangladeshis are loud" or "Italians are chaotic" or "Germans are uptight" without pausing to add "well, technically I mean tendency not trait." That's just normal shorthand, and I'm not going to pretend everyday speech works like a philosophy seminar. But casual phrasing being common doesn't mean the underlying distinction stops mattering — it just means people are being imprecise, the same way people say "it's freezing" without meaning literally 32°F. The looseness of everyday language is fine for describing weather. It's less fine when the shorthand is a judgment about an entire ethnicity's character, because that's exactly the situation where the sloppy version and the precise version lead to different real-world behavior — one is "huh, interesting, communication style is different here," the other can slide into "I should expect less of this person" or "this group is just like that" when you're dealing with an actual individual. So sure, nobody talks like a philosophy class in casual conversation. But you specifically have spent this whole conversation defending the strong, literal version of the claim — not "yeah I was just using loose shorthand, I don't really mean it as a fixed trait." If it really was just casual shorthand for you, this would've been a much shorter conversation.
